arred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2021-05-08T01:40:52Z","updated_at":"2021-05-08T01:40:52Z","body":"well that idea seems to work just fine for something simple at least, nice. \r\n\r\n```python\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import ForeignKey\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import Integer\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import contains_eager\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import relationship\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import Session\r\n\r\nBase = declarative_base()\r\n\r\n\r\nclass A(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = \"a\"\r\n\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n    b_id = Column(ForeignKey(\"b.id\"))\r\n    b = relationship(\"B\")\r\n\r\n\r\nclass B(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = \"b\"\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n    cs = relationship(\"C\")\r\n\r\n\r\nclass C(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = \"c`\"\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n    b_id = Column(ForeignKey(\"b.id\"))\r\n\r\n\r\ne = create_engine(\"sqlite://\", echo=True)\r\nBase.metadata.create_all(e)\r\n\r\na1 = A(b=B(cs=[C(), C()]))\r\n\r\ns = Session(e)\r\n\r\ns.add(a1)\r\ns.commit()\r\ns.close()\r\n\r\n\r\na1 = s.query(A).join(B).options(contains_eager(A.b).joinedload(B.cs)).first()\r\nprint(a1.b.cs)```","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/comments/834944905/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"zzzeek","id":128223,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyODIyMw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/128223?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek","html_url":"https://github.com/zzzeek","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zzzeek/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/comments/835457338","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/6449#issuecomment-835457338","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/6449","id":835457338,"node_id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDgzNTQ1NzMzOA==","user":{"login":"awgymer","id":24782660,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0NzgyNjYw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24782660?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer","html_url":"https://github.com/awgymer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2021-05-08T18:17:45Z","updated_at":"2021-05-08T18:18:11Z","body":"Ok so you're right, it can't be replicated with my code above, apologies. I have just discovered that it doesn't actually error on the `commit` but rather when you then try to access the results in the query *after* the update. \r\n\r\nThe below should be a fully reproducible example. \r\n\r\n```py\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import ForeignKey\r\nfrom sqlalchemy import Integer\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import contains_eager\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import relationship\r\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import Session\r\n\r\nBase = declarative_base()\r\n\r\n\r\nclass Part(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = \"part\"\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n\r\n\r\nclass Color(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = \"color\"\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n\r\n\r\nclass IPart(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = \"ipart\"\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n    p_id = Column(ForeignKey(\"part.id\"))\r\n    c_id = Column(ForeignKey(\"color.id\"))\r\n    color = relationship('Color')\r\n    part = relationship('Part')\r\n\r\n\r\nclass UPart(Base):\r\n    __tablename__ = \"upart\"\r\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\r\n    i_id = Column(ForeignKey(\"ipart.id\"))\r\n    num_missing = Column(Integer, server_default='0')\r\n    part = relationship('IPart')\r\n\r\n\r\ne = create_engine(\"sqlite:///./test.sqlite\", echo=True)\r\n\r\n\r\nBase.metadata.create_all(e)\r\n\r\ns = Session(e)\r\n\r\np1 = Part()\r\nc1 = Color()\r\nc2 = Color()\r\n\r\ns.add(p1)\r\ns.add(c1)\r\ns.add(c2)\r\ns.commit()\r\n\r\ni1 = IPart(p_id=p1.id, c_id=c2.id)\r\ni2 = IPart(p_id=p1.id, c_id=c1.id)\r\n\r\ns.add(i1)\r\ns.add(i2)\r\ns.commit()\r\n\r\nu1 = UPart(i_id=i1.id)\r\nu2 = UPart(i_id=i1.id)\r\nu3 = UPart(i_id=i2.id)\r\ns.add(u1)\r\ns.add(u2)\r\ns.add(u3)\r\ns.commit()\r\ns.close()\r\n\r\ns = Session(e)\r\n\r\nq = s.query(UPart).join(IPart).\\\r\n    filter(UPart.id != 1).\\\r\n    order_by(IPart.c_id).\\\r\n    options(\r\n        contains_eager(UPart.part).joinedload(IPart.color),\r\n        contains_eager(UPart.part).joinedload(IPart.part)\r\n    )\r\n\r\nr = q.all()\r\n\r\nprint(r[0].num_missing)\r\nprint(r[0].part.color.id)\r\n\r\nupdate_query = s.query(UPart).\\\r\n    filter(UPart.id != 1).update(\r\n    {\"num_missing\": 1}, synchronize_session=\"fetch\"\r\n)\r\n\r\ns.commit()\r\n\r\nprint(r[0].part)\r\n```\r\n\r\nNote that closing the session in which you create the objects and opening a new session to run the queries in seems to be key in reproducing this bug. In my real-world example obviously the models I am retrieving from the DB were put there by totally separate code. \r\n\r\nI'll also include the last few lines of output.\r\n\r\n```\r\n2021-05-08 19:11:21,881 INFO sqlalchemy.engine.Engine SELECT upart.id \r\nFROM upart \r\nWHERE upart.id != ?\r\n2021-05-08 19:11:21,881 INFO sqlalchemy.engine.Engine [generated in 0.00019s] (1,)\r\n2021-05-08 19:11:21,882 INFO sqlalchemy.engine.Engine UPDATE upart SET num_missing=? WHERE upart.id != ?\r\n2021-05-08 19:11:21,882 INFO sqlalchemy.engine.Engine [generated in 0.00009s] (1, 1)\r\n2021-05-08 19:11:21,882 INFO sqlalchemy.engine.Engine COMMIT\r\n2021-05-08 19:11:21,883 INFO sqlalchemy.engine.Engine BEGIN (implicit)\r\n2021-05-08 19:11:21,884 INFO sqlalchemy.engine.Engine SELECT ipart.id AS ipart_id, ipart.p_id AS ipart_p_id, ipart.c_id AS ipart_c_id, upart.id AS upart_id, upart.i_id AS upart_i_id, upart.num_missing AS upart_num_missing, color_1.id AS color_1_id, part_1.id AS part_1_id \r\nFROM ipart, upart LEFT OUTER JOIN color AS color_1 ON color_1.id = ipart.c_id LEFT OUTER JOIN part AS part_1 ON part_1.id = ipart.p_id \r\nWHERE upart.id = ?\r\n2021-05-08 19:11:21,884 INFO sqlalchemy.engine.Engine [generated in 0.00009s] (3,)\r\n/Users/arthurgymer/.pyenv/versions/lego/lib/python3.8/site-packages/sqlalchemy/orm/strategies.py:2580: SAWarning: Multiple rows returned with uselist=False for eagerly-loaded attribute 'UPart.part' \r\n  util.warn(\r\n<__main__.IPart object at 0x104c328b0>\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nAs for the:\r\n```\r\ncontains_eager(UserPart.part).joinedload(InventoryPart.part),\r\ncontains_eager(UserPart.part).joinedload(InventoryPart.color)\r\n```\r\n It seems to work fine. It's not the issue here either so far as I can tell, I removed it and nothing changed. My aim with that code was to eager-load *both* the `part` and `color` relationships on the eagerly-loaded `UserPart.part`. The docs show use of chained `joinedload` for 1 eagerly loaded child and 1 eagerly loaded grandchild, but not for 2 grandchildren. I tried a few things but this was the only one that seemed to work. The SQL issued by the query looks reasonable too.    ","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/comments/835457338/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"awgymer","id":24782660,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0NzgyNjYw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24782660?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer","html_url":"https://github.com/awgymer","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/awgymer/repos","events_ur